# Review: ScriptPlugin

The editor, the create flow and the skill are built. `Script` (from `@dxos/compute`) is registered.
The navtree creates it through `CreateScript`, seeded from the first template, a chosen template
or a Gist. `ScriptArticle` runs a TypeScript CodeMirror editor bound to the source `Text` with the
configured input mode. The Execute and Logs companions are in the app graph. The `org.dxos.skill.script`
skill carries all nine operations with handlers.

The UI deploy and invoke paths diverge from the spec. The toolbar Deploy button deploys directly
through `deployScript` and shows a generic error item and a copy-link action. It never opens
`DeploymentDialog`, which only creates and deploys template scripts after a token is added, and
nothing in the repo opens it. The Execute panel is a free-text JSON input with a TODO to become a
schema form, and it calls `FunctionsServiceClient.invoke` rather than `op:invoke`. The skill ops
take a `PersistentOperation` ref, not a `Script` ref, and `installFunction` creates a
`PersistentOperation`, not a Script. `Notebook` holds inline typed cells (`markdown`, `script`,
`query`, `prompt`, `view`) with `Text` sources, not linked Scripts. The schema module does not
register it.

Testing is thin. `plugin.test.ts` checks module activation. The compiler and notebook-parser tests
cover internals. The two deploy e2e suites are `describe.skip`. No test invokes a skill op.

Of 56 items: 0 done, 26 untested, 10 partial, 11 diverged, 9 missing.

